{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790330730,"nonce":"kZSW6V7J3FO5goqLWr4JDViS8c8=","title":"Foreign objects: the embedding program's values in Lua and Python","message":"An embedder (caribou) needs its programs in Lua and Python to reach the rest of its world: modules, classes, objects and plugins of other languages. Caribou issue 64448a6c563dea6d3c1e3539e791176879fc0587f56919cff7c9fe627f7cfda4 tracks its side.\n\nA foreign object is a box of a new custom kind, FOREIGN_TAG, whose data is a word the embedder chose and whose dropper hands the word back. zyntax_embed::foreign holds the Foreign trait the embedder implements (get, set, call, invoke, text, type name, equality, hash, import, release), one install slot per process like the fiber backend, and the $Foreign$* symbols as a static plugin both frontends register. The shared library wraps them (zb_foreign_*), raises what the embedder reports as a library error of the kind it names, and handles the kind in type, truthiness, str, equality and hash.\n\nLua sees one as userdata: indexing reads or writes the embedder's member, calling calls it, so o:m() is the index giving the method called with o first; tostring and == ask the embedder; require asks the embedder after package.path.\n\nPython reaches one through its dynamic paths: the generated getattr, setattr and method dispatchers, and zb_call_N. An import of a module the program does not have binds __import__(\"a.b\") (or its member), which asks the embedder and raises ModuleNotFoundError when it has none; ImportError and ModuleNotFoundError join the prelude.\n\nTyped externs for Python (host classes with declared signatures, checked at compile time) build on this; filed separately.","files":null}]}